  abstract = {We formally study how {\textbackslash}emph\{ensemble\} of deep learning models can improve test accuracy, and how the superior performance of ensemble can be distilled into a single model using {\textbackslash}emph\{knowledge distillation\}. We consider the challenging case where the ensemble is simply an average of the outputs of a few independently trained neural networks with the {\textbackslash}emph\{same\} architecture, trained using the {\textbackslash}emph\{same\} algorithm on the {\textbackslash}emph\{same\} data set, and they only differ by the random seeds used in the initialization. We show that ensemble/knowledge distillation in {\textbackslash}emph\{deep learning\} works very differently from traditional learning theory (such as boosting or NTKs). We develop a theory showing that when data has a structure we refer to as ``multi-view'', then ensemble of independently trained neural networks can provably improve test accuracy, and such superior test accuracy can also be provably distilled into a single model. Our result sheds light on how ensemble works in deep learning in a way that is completely different from traditional theorems, and how the ``dark knowledge'' is hidden in the outputs of the ensemble and can be used in distillation.},
  langid = {english},
  file = {C:\Users\furuh\Zotero\storage\GFEQXFVQ\Allen-Zhu and Li - 2022 - Towards Understanding Ensemble, Knowledge Distilla.pdf}
