  abstract = {This work proposes a general and scalable approximate sampling strategy for probabilistic models with discrete variables that outperforms variational auto-encoders and existing energy-based models and gives bounds showing that this approach is near-optimal in the class of samplers which propose local updates. We propose a general and scalable approximate sampling strategy for probabilistic models with discrete variables. Our approach uses gradients of the likelihood function with respect to its discrete inputs to propose updates in a MetropolisHastings sampler. We show empirically that this approach outperforms generic samplers in a number of difficult settings including Ising models, Potts models, restricted Boltzmann machines, and factorial hidden Markov models. We also demonstrate the use of our improved sampler for training deep energy-based models (EBM) on high dimensional discrete data. This approach outperforms variational auto-encoders and existing energy-based models. Finally, we give bounds showing that our approach is near-optimal in the class of samplers which propose local updates.},
