  abstract = {Despite many applications, dimensionality reduction in the \${\textbackslash}ell\_1\$-norm is much less understood than in the Euclidean norm. We give two new oblivious dimensionality reduction techniques for the \${\textbackslash}ell\_1\$-norm which improve exponentially over prior ones: 1. We design a distribution over random matrices \$S {\textbackslash}in {\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\}{\textasciicircum}\{r {\textbackslash}times n\}\$, where \$r = 2{\textasciicircum}\{{\textbackslash}tilde O(d/({\textbackslash}varepsilon {\textbackslash}delta))\}\$, such that given any matrix \$A {\textbackslash}in {\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\}{\textasciicircum}\{n {\textbackslash}times d\}\$, with probability at least \$1-{\textbackslash}delta\$, simultaneously for all \$x\$, \${\textbackslash}{\textbar}SAx{\textbackslash}{\textbar}\_1 = (1 {\textbackslash}pm {\textbackslash}varepsilon){\textbackslash}{\textbar}Ax{\textbackslash}{\textbar}\_1\$. Note that \$S\$ is linear, does not depend on \$A\$, and maps \${\textbackslash}ell\_1\$ into \${\textbackslash}ell\_1\$. Our distribution provides an exponential improvement on the previous best known map of Wang and Woodruff (SODA, 2019), which required \$r = 2{\textasciicircum}\{2{\textasciicircum}\{{\textbackslash}Omega(d)\}\}\$, even for constant \${\textbackslash}varepsilon\$ and \${\textbackslash}delta\$. Our bound is optimal, up to a polynomial factor in the exponent, given a known \$2{\textasciicircum}\{{\textbackslash}sqrt d\}\$ lower bound for constant \${\textbackslash}varepsilon\$ and \${\textbackslash}delta\$. 2. We design a distribution over matrices \$S {\textbackslash}in {\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\}{\textasciicircum}\{k {\textbackslash}times n\}\$, where \$k = 2{\textasciicircum}\{O(q{\textasciicircum}2)\}({\textbackslash}varepsilon{\textasciicircum}\{-1\} q {\textbackslash}log d){\textasciicircum}\{O(q)\}\$, such that given any \$q\$-mode tensor \$A {\textbackslash}in ({\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\}{\textasciicircum}\{d\}){\textasciicircum}\{{\textbackslash}otimes q\}\$, one can estimate the entrywise \${\textbackslash}ell\_1\$-norm \${\textbackslash}{\textbar}A{\textbackslash}{\textbar}\_1\$ from \$S(A)\$. Moreover, \$S = S{\textasciicircum}1 {\textbackslash}otimes S{\textasciicircum}2 {\textbackslash}otimes {\textbackslash}cdots {\textbackslash}otimes S{\textasciicircum}q\$ and so given vectors \$u\_1, {\textbackslash}ldots, u\_q {\textbackslash}in {\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\}{\textasciicircum}d\$, one can compute \$S(u\_1 {\textbackslash}otimes u\_2 {\textbackslash}otimes {\textbackslash}cdots {\textbackslash}otimes u\_q)\$ in time \$2{\textasciicircum}\{O(q{\textasciicircum}2)\}({\textbackslash}varepsilon{\textasciicircum}\{-1\} q {\textbackslash}log d){\textasciicircum}\{O(q)\}\$, which is much faster than the \$d{\textasciicircum}q\$ time required to form \$u\_1 {\textbackslash}otimes u\_2 {\textbackslash}otimes {\textbackslash}cdots {\textbackslash}otimes u\_q\$. Our linear map gives a streaming algorithm for independence testing using space \$2{\textasciicircum}\{O(q{\textasciicircum}2)\}({\textbackslash}varepsilon{\textasciicircum}\{-1\} q {\textbackslash}log d){\textasciicircum}\{O(q)\}\$, improving the previous doubly exponential \$({\textbackslash}varepsilon{\textasciicircum}\{-1\} {\textbackslash}log d){\textasciicircum}\{q{\textasciicircum}\{O(q)\}\}\$ space bound of Braverman and Ostrovsky (STOC, 2010).},
