  journal = {ArXiv},
  urldate = {2024-07-19},
  abstract = {We propose a new provable method for robust PCA, where the task is to recover a low-rank matrix, which is corrupted with sparse perturbations. Our method consists of simple alternating projections onto the set of low rank and sparse matrices with intermediate de-noising steps. We prove correct recovery of the low rank and sparse components under tight recovery conditions, which match those for the state-of-art convex relaxation techniques. Our method is extremely simple to implement and has low computational complexity. For a \$m {\textbackslash}times n\$ input matrix (say m {\textbackslash}geq n), our method has O(r{\textasciicircum}2 mn{\textbackslash}log(1/{\textbackslash}epsilon)) running time, where \$r\$ is the rank of the low-rank component and \${\textbackslash}epsilon\$ is the accuracy. In contrast, the convex relaxation methods have a running time O(mn{\textasciicircum}2/{\textbackslash}epsilon), which is not scalable to large problem instances. Our running time nearly matches that of the usual PCA (i.e. non robust), which is O(rmn{\textbackslash}log (1/{\textbackslash}epsilon)). Thus, we achieve ``best of both the worlds'', viz low computational complexity and provable recovery for robust PCA. Our analysis represents one of the few instances of global convergence guarantees for non-convex methods.},
